  "worked_solution": "Since $x=-10$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+10)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+10)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+10)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+10)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-10$ | $1$ | $30$ | $300$ | $1000$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $-10$ | $-200$ | $-1000$ |\n|  | $1$ | $20$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x+10}={x}^{2}+20x+100,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x+10)({x}^{2}+20x+100).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+10)(x+10)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+10)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+10)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-10$ has multiplicity $3.$",
